27418 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Have you ever seen a scene in a film or television where kidnappers demand a ransom\077 The criminals on screen no longer ask for bags of cash\056 They ask for Bitcoin\056 Embedded within that brief scene is a familiar assumption\072 no names\054 no identities\054 and no easy way to trace the money\056 Most audiences rarely question that assumption\056 The belief that crypto is untraceable money has already become part of popular common sense\056\n\nThat perception was not simply a popular misunderstanding\056 For the libertarians and cypherpunks who shaped the early crypto ecosystem\054 anonymity was central to the design itself\056 Crypto emerged as a technological expression of the belief that individuals should be able to protect their economic freedom from the control of states and central banks\056 The drug vendors of Silk Road chose Bitcoin for that reason\056 Ransomware groups demanded payment in crypto for the same reason\056 North Korea turned stolen crypto assets into a source of state revenue based on that same belief\056 For a time\054 the belief even appeared to hold true in reality\056\n\nYet that gap quietly \024 and rapidly \024 began to narrow\056 As blockchain analytics technologies emerged\054 it became possible to trace the flow of funds hidden behind anonymous wallet addresses\054 analyze transaction patterns\054 and connect specific addresses to real organizations or individuals\056\n\nIn 2018\054 Jeongwoo Son\054 the operator of the world\047s largest child sexual exploitation website\054 Welcome to Video\054 was arrested\056 Operating through Bitcoin payments\054 the site was ultimately dismantled through an international investigation spanning 38 countries\054 leading to the arrest of 337 individuals\056 The U\056S\056 Internal Revenue Service Criminal Investigation division \050IRS\055CI\051 played a key role by tracing Bitcoin transaction flows and identifying critical leads that helped pinpoint the operator\056 Following international cooperation\054 Son was arrested by South Korean police\0561\051 The crime had operated on the belief that Bitcoin was anonymous money\056 In the end\054 it collapsed because of the very records that money left behind\056\n\nStates still remain uncomfortable with crypto\056 And complete control remains impossible\056 But states now know that\054 when necessary\054 crypto can be traced and blocked\056 Governments around the world began casting regulatory nets over the crypto ecosystem\056 Crypto\054 once seen as existing beyond the reach of the state\054 is now being reframed in terms acceptable to institutional finance\056 Where did that confidence come from\077\n\nThis report follows that question\056 It examines how the maturation of blockchain analytics technologies became one of the central conditions for the institutionalization of crypto\056 It also explores how the logic that \042what can be analyzed can be managed\054 and what can be managed can be institutionalized\042 fundamentally transformed the landscape surrounding crypto\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 27419 0 R
     /Pg 1640 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

