27439 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Blockchain appears anonymous and yet entirely public at the same time\056 Every transaction is permanently recorded on a public ledger that anyone can inspect\056 All transactions occurring on the Bitcoin network can be queried in real time through a blockchain explorer\056 Every record of when a particular wallet address sent funds\054 how much\054 and to which address is disclosed with full transparency\056 This transparency is a core design principle of blockchain\056 All participants share the same ledger in order to prevent double\055spending and guarantee the integrity of transactions\056\n\nYet this transparency is deliberately only half\055open\056 Transaction records are visible\054 but the parties behind those transactions are not\056 On the blockchain\054 users exist not as names or account numbers but solely as strings of characters called wallet addresses\056 Addresses in the form of 1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7DivfNa stand in for the sender and recipient of a transaction\056 Nowhere in the ledger is there any record of who owns that address or what purpose it serves\056 The data exists\054 but without context\056\n\nThis is precisely where the paradox arises\056 Blockchain is a transparent ledger that makes every transaction public\054 yet it tells you nothing about whose transactions those are\056 Transparency and pseudonymity coexist within a single structure\056 The transaction records are fully disclosed\054 but the ability to convert those records into meaningful information \024 the ability to read them \024 is not given to everyone\056 In an environment where millions of wallet addresses and billions of transaction data points pour forth\054 determining who owns a particular address\054 or which flows of funds are connected to criminal activity\054 is in practice extremely difficult without sophisticated technology and vast data\056 All can see it\054 but not all can read it\056\n\nThe political scientist James C\056 Scott\054 in his book Seeing Like a State\054 observed that for a state to govern society it must first convert it into a \042legible form\056\042 To manage a forest\054 trees must be classified and surveyed\073 to manage a population\054 surnames and addresses must be standardized\056 Abstracting a complex reality into a simplified form that the state can perceive and intervene in \024 that\054 he argued\054 is the starting point of governance\0562\051 The key point in Scott\047s logic is that visibility is not simply a matter of collecting information\056 Making something readable is an act of converting it into a manageable object\054 and it is at that moment that the governing power of the state begins to operate\056 What cannot be seen cannot be managed\054 and what cannot be managed cannot be institutionalized\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 27440 0 R
     /Pg 1980 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

