27556 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Chainalysis was founded in 2014 in the aftermath of the Mt\056 Gox hack\0568\051 When approximately 850\054000 BTC vanished from what was then the world\047s largest Bitcoin exchange\054 the incident threw into sharp relief the need for specialized tools capable of visualizing and tracing blockchain transactions\056 Chainalysis was among the first companies to meet that need in a systematic and scalable way\056\n\nFrom its earliest years\054 the company built cooperative relationships with major U\056S\056 law\055enforcement and regulatory agencies\054 including the FBI and the IRS\0549\051 establishing a powerful first\055mover advantage that soon expanded into a network effect\056 Because Chainalysis had accumulated and labeled vast volumes of on\055chain data ahead of its competitors\054 a virtuous cycle took hold\072 the more institutions used Chainalysis\054 the more refined its data became\073 the more refined its data became\054 the more institutions chose Chainalysis\056 In practice\054 Chainalysis\047s exchange customers share tens of thousands of wallet addresses with the company every day\054 enabling real\055time verification of clustering accuracy\056 This self\055reinforcing data loop creates a competitive advantage that rival latecomers cannot easily close\056 Today\054 Chainalysis is widely regarded as the dominant firm in the global blockchain analytics market\054 with more than 1\054500 organizations using its platform worldwide\05610\051\n\nGeopolitics also plays a decisive role in this position\056 For U\056S\056 agencies such as the FBI and the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network \050FinCEN\051\054 relying on infrastructure provided by an American company is a natural choice from the perspectives of data sovereignty and legal jurisdiction\056 In a world where the United States occupies the center of the global financial system\054 analytical frameworks adopted as standards by U\056S\056 institutions often become de facto global standards\056 The fact that investigative agencies and exchanges in South Korea\054 Europe\054 and Japan routinely cooperate using Chainalysis data is a direct consequence of this structure\056\n\nAs a result\054 private blockchain analytics firms such as Chainalysis have evolved beyond the role of technology vendors\056 They now function as public infrastructure upon which states depend in order to govern and manage crypto\056 As with Palantir\054 private technology has become an instrument of public power\056 The same pattern is now unfolding in the blockchain sector\056 Only when this structure is in place do the conditions for the institutionalization of crypto fully emerge\056\n\nThat said\054 blockchain analytics technology alone has its limits\056 For ordinary transactions conducted through exchanges\054 combining KYC \050Know Your Customer\051 information with on\055chain data is sufficient to identify the individuals involved\056 The problem arises with sophisticated criminals who conceal their identities thoroughly and bypass exchanges altogether\056 In such cases\054 off\055chain information \024 law enforcement HUMINT\054 devices obtained through search and seizure\054 testimony from victims who have escaped \024 becomes the first link connecting a specific wallet address to a real person\056 The moment that link is secured\054 blockchain analytics operates as an amplifier capable of tracing flows of funds worth tens of billions of dollars\056 How this combination of off\055chain and on\055chain intelligence actually works in practice can be seen in the following chapter\047s case study of Prince Group chairman Chen Zhi\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 27557 0 R
     /Pg 4565 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

