27879 0 obj
  << /ActualText (For the state to institutionalize something\054 it must first be able to see it\056 Historically\054 states have been unable to manage what they cannot see\056 Underground economies\054 informal transactions\054 black markets \024 the reason these economic activities remain outside the institutional framework is not simply that they are illegal\054 but that the state has been unable to grasp their flows\056 Institutionalization begins with visibility\056 In its early days\054 crypto was a domain invisible to the state\056 Transactions were taking place\054 but it was impossible to know who was sending what to whom\056 The reason the state was uncomfortable with crypto was not simply because of the ideology of decentralization\056 It was because crypto could not be seen\056 What cannot be seen cannot be managed\054 and what cannot be managed cannot be institutionalized\056\n\nThe emergence of blockchain analytics technology began to transform crypto into something the state could read\056 The figure who most symbolically embodies this transformation in the field of investigation is Kathryn Haun\05635\051 In 2012\054 Haun \024 then a federal prosecutor at the U\056S\056 Department of Justice \024 took on a Bitcoin investigation and made a decisive discovery\056 The blockchain was a public ledger that permanently recorded every transaction\054 and these records left traces that were in fact easier to trace than cash or international wire transfers\056 Haun called this \042digital breadcrumbs\056\04236\051)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 27880 0 R
     /Pg 11168 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

