27943 0 obj
  << /ActualText (2023\054 is the world\047s first comprehensive crypto regulatory framework\056 MiCA requires all crypto\055asset service providers \050CASPs\051 operating in Europe to establish KYC obligations\054 internal controls\054 and risk management frameworks\056 The Transfer of Funds Regulation \050TFR\051 mandates that sender and recipient information be transmitted with every virtual asset transfer\05642\051 The real\055time transaction monitoring\054 risk assessment\054 and suspicious transaction reporting that MiCA demands are functions already provided by blockchain analytics solutions such as Chainalysis\047s KYT and Elliptic\047s holistic screening\056 Regulation has legislated into law what analytics technology had already put into practice\056\n\nIf technology opened the door to regulation\054 regulation in turn created demand for technology\056 During the legislative processes for the Travel Rule and MiCA\054 blockchain analytics firms actively shaped public discourse on the meaning and direction of regulation\054 exercising considerable influence\056 Elliptic\047s Vice President of Policy and Regulatory Affairs David Carlisle analyzed the fact that the EU Travel Rule applies to all crypto transactions beyond FATF standards\054 presenting a framework for the industry\047s response\073 TRM Labs\047 Head of Legal and Government Affairs Ari Redbord characterized MiCA as \042the most comprehensive legal framework for crypto in the world\054\042 lending support to the legitimacy of the regulation\056 Before joining TRM Labs\054 Redbord had served as Senior Advisor to the U\056S\056 Deputy Secretary of the Treasury and the Under Secretary for Terrorism and Financial Intelligence\05643\051 This flow of personnel \024 in which key figures from regulatory authorities move into private blockchain analytics firms \024 is a telling illustration of a structure of co\055evolution in which technology and institutions reinforce each other through policy and discourse as well\056\n\nThe spot Bitcoin ETF was approved by the U\056S\056 Securities and Exchange Commission \050SEC\051 in January 2024\056 The core reason the SEC had refused to approve a spot ETF for years was concern over market manipulation\056 The situation changed when Grayscale filed suit in the U\056S\056 Court of Appeals for the D\056C\056 Circuit\056 The court ruled that the SEC\047s logic in permitting Bitcoin futures ETFs while rejecting spot ETFs was inconsistent\054 and the SEC was effectively compelled to grant approval by the court\047s decision\05644\051 Yet underlying that approval was the fact that the surveillability of the crypto market had materially improved\056 As blockchain analytics solutions from Chainalysis\054 TRM Labs\054 and others were adopted across exchanges\054 the premise that had long underpinned the SEC\047s objections \024 that the market was unsurveillable \024 was being eroded\056 If the court opened the door\054 blockchain analytics technology had been creating the conditions under which that door could be opened\056\n\nIn July 2025\054 the GENIUS Act \024 the United States\047 first federal stablecoin regulatory law \024       \176)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 27944 0 R
     /Pg 12730 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

