27997 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Satoshi Nakamoto\047s 2008 white paper was written in a particular vocabulary\056 Terms like decentralization\054 censorship resistance\054 trustless transactions\054 and peer\055to\055peer payments were not mere technical terminology\056 They were a political declaration that compressed within itself a distrust of states and financial institutions\054 a conviction that individuals should have full sovereignty over their own assets\054 and a resistance to existing power structures\056 The early crypto community cohered around this shared vocabulary\056 To buy Bitcoin was not simply an investment \024 it was an ideological choice\056\n\nThat vocabulary has not entirely disappeared\056 But the vocabulary of mainstream discourse around crypto has changed completely\056 KYC\054 AML\054 the Travel Rule\054 sanctions compliance \024 these are the terms the crypto industry now uses constantly\056 To operate an institutional exchange\054 KYC must be in place\073 for institutions to handle crypto\054 AML frameworks must be built\073 cross\055border transfers are subject to the Travel Rule\056 The vocabulary of crypto has shifted from the vocabulary of resistance to the vocabulary of regulatory compliance\056\n\nThis shift is not coincidental\056 As blockchain analytics technology made crypto visible\054 the state was able to impose the framework of institutionalization \024 and as that framework was imposed\054 the industry\054 seeking to survive within it\054 adopted a new vocabulary\056 Vocabulary follows power\056 The fact that the vocabulary of crypto has changed means that the power structure surrounding crypto has changed\056)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 27998 0 R
     /Pg 13370 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

