28012 0 obj
  << /ActualText (That blockchain analytics firms are more than mere technology service providers was already established in Chapter 2\056 But the nature of the power these firms hold goes beyond simple market dominance\056 They effectively exercise the authority to define what is good and what is evil in the crypto ecosystem\056\n\nConsider Chainalysis\047s labeling framework\056 When a particular wallet address is labeled as ransomware\055linked\054 darknet market\055associated\054 or sanctions\055designated\054 every transaction connected to that address is classified as high\055risk\056 Exchanges refuse deposits of the relevant funds\054 investigators begin tracing\054 and through integration with OFAC sanctions the address is blocked from the global financial network\056 Conversely\054 addresses labeled as exchange hot wallets or legitimate services are incorporated as part of the normal financial system\056 A single label determines the fate of funds\056\n\nYet the criteria and algorithms behind this labeling are protected as trade secrets and are not disclosed to the outside world\056 There is no way for anyone outside to verify who classified a particular address as dangerous\054 on what basis\054 or whether that judgment was right or wrong\056 When false positives occur\054 the assets of innocent individuals can be frozen \024 but accountability is unclear\056 It is a paradox in which a technology designed for transparency produces an opaque structure of judgment\056\n\nThis structure also carries geopolitical implications\056 Chainalysis is an American company\054 and its data and labeling framework sit within U\056S\056 legal jurisdiction\056 OFAC\047s sanctions list is                \176)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 28013 0 R
     /Pg 13670 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

