28046 0 obj
  << /ActualText (South Korea is a powerhouse in virtual assets\056 In 2025\054 the daily average trading volume of domestic virtual assets exceeded five trillion won \050approximately \0443\0566 billion\051\05647\051 Yet the infrastructure for monitoring and tracing that enormous volume of transactions comes not from South Korea but from the United States\056\n\nDomestic exchanges have also attempted to build independent infrastructure for Travel Rule compliance\056 CODE \024 established jointly by Bithumb\054 Coinone\054 and Korbit \024 is a representative example\054 but CODE operates by outsourcing its core AML analysis to TRM Labs through a strategic partnership\05648\051 The outward form of independent infrastructure has been achieved\054 but the core capability of actually analyzing fund flows and assessing risk remains delegated to an American firm\056 This structure reveals that South Korean exchanges have adopted the form of compliance\054 but the substantive judgment that fills that form depends on external parties\056\n\nThe Financial Intelligence Unit \050FIU\051 also announced in its 2026 work plan that it would introduce an AI\055based analytics system and utilize Chainalysis as a virtual asset analytics tool\05649\051 The effort to strengthen analytical capability is positive in itself\054 but the structure of dependence on a U\056S\056 private firm for the core analytics tool is no different from the situation of the exchanges\056\n\nThe situation of investigative agencies is more serious\056 Over the five years from 2021 to 2025\054 losses from domestic virtual asset crime reached approximately seven trillion won\054 yet the assets actually seized by investigative agencies amounted to just 0\0567 percent of that figure\05650\051 This number plainly reflects the structural limitation that without independent on\055chain                  \056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 28047 0 R
     /Pg 14908 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

