28087 0 obj
  << /ActualText (South Korea\047s crypto regulatory framework is built around two pieces of legislation\056 The amendment to the Special Financial Information Act in March 2021 introduced a registration system for virtual asset service providers and anti\055money laundering obligations\056 The Virtual Asset User Protection Act\054 which came into force in July 2024\054 added user asset protection and regulations against market manipulation\05651\051 The direction this regulatory framework points in is singular\072 protecting investors and blocking illegal transactions \024 in other words\054 defense\056\n\nThe limits of defensive regulation become sharper when viewed against the backdrop of the global crypto order\056 As examined above\054 the United States is accumulating Bitcoin as a national strategic asset and has legislated the dollar stablecoin ecosystem through the GENIUS Act\056 Movement in Asia is equally clear\056 In April 2026\054 the Japanese government passed an amendment to the Financial Instruments and Exchange Act\054 formally designating virtual assets as financial instruments and committing to the introduction of spot virtual asset ETFs by 2028\056 It also plans to lower the tax rate on virtual asset investment income from the current maximum of 55 percent to a flat rate of 20 percent\054 expanding individual investor participation in the market\05652\051\n\nHong Kong approved spot Bitcoin and Ethereum ETFs in April 2024 \024 the first in Asia\056 The         \176)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 28088 0 R
     /Pg 15563 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
