28107 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Ethereum spot ETF approval was also a world first\054 placing Hong Kong ahead of the United States\056 With China maintaining its ban on virtual asset trading\054 Hong Kong announced in 2022 its ambition to become the center of the virtual asset market\054 and has been refining its regulatory framework since June 2023 \024 when it launched a licensing regime for virtual asset trading operators and permitted individual investor participation\05653\051\n\nDubai established the Virtual Assets Regulatory Authority \050VARA\051 in 2022 \024 the world\047s first dedicated virtual asset regulator \024 and has been actively attracting global virtual asset firms using regulatory clarity and tax incentives as its competitive weapons\056 From 2022 to 2025\054 the number of registered virtual asset businesses in Dubai quadrupled from 10 to 39\056 Their approach to regulation is not mere defense but a strategic act of designing order\056 South Korea\054 by contrast\054 has remained in a defensive posture that keeps virtual assets and finance separate\056 As the migration of global blockchain firms to the Middle East has become a clear trend\054 South Korea\047s institutional environment has been unable to stem the outflow of domestic operators\05654\051 Indeed\054 while the number of registered crypto businesses in Dubai grew from 10 to 39\054 the number in South Korea fell from 42 to 27 \024 a decline of roughly 36 percent\056\n\nDiscussion of introducing spot virtual asset ETFs in South Korea has yet to gain any serious momentum\054 and derivatives trading remains restricted\056 As a result\054 the flow of domestic investment capital to overseas exchanges with more flexible regulatory environments is accelerating\056 In 2025\054 funds transferred by domestic investors to overseas exchanges were estimated at approximately 160 trillion won \050approximately \044115 billion\051 annually\05655\051 Transactions are happening in South Korea\054 but the money is flowing out\056\n\nWhile South Korea\047s regulation has been focused on investor protection\054 the standards of the global crypto order are hardening \024 led by the United States\054 with Dubai emerging as a new hub\056 Despite its enormous trading volume\054 South Korea is structurally excluded from participating in the design of that order\056 There is regulation\054 but no strategy\056 This is the core limitation of South Korea\047s crypto regulatory framework\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 28108 0 R
     /Pg 16321 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

