  << /ActualText (domestic exchanges and investigative agencies can jointly utilize\056 As examined above\054 domestic exchanges are outsourcing the core capabilities of blockchain analytics to American firms\056 In this structure\054 the more data accumulates\054 the more it is the analytical capability of American firms that is strengthened\056 If domestic exchanges were to jointly accumulate on\055chain transaction data and cooperate with investigative agencies to build a labeling framework suited to the domestic context\054 the foundation for reducing long\055term dependence on American firms would be laid\056 The fact that cooperative structures such as CODE already exist can serve as a starting point\056 In this process\054 the Financial Intelligence Unit \050FIU\051 needs to make a role transition \024 moving beyond simple regulatory oversight to function as a domestic on\055chain data hub\056\n\nThe U\056S\056 FinCEN functions as a central data hub that integrates and analyzes data collected from investigative agencies\054 financial institutions\054 and overseas FIUs\054 providing actionable intelligence to law enforcement\05656\051 Australia\047s financial intelligence agency AUSTRAC\054 through its public\055private partnership program Fintel Alliance\054 operates a system in which major banks\054 remittance providers\054 and investigative agencies share and analyze financial intelligence on an integrated platform\054 jointly producing financial crime intelligence including on virtual assets\05657\051 If South Korea\047s FIU were equipped with a similarly integrated on\055chain data analysis function\054 the core judgment authority currently delegated to American firms could be progressively internalized\056\n\nThe second direction is to build a regulatory framework that meets global standards\054 but designed in the vocabulary of strategy rather than defense\056 Travel Rule compliance and the construction of an anti\055money laundering framework are necessary conditions \024 but they are not sufficient\056 Just as the United States has designed the dollar stablecoin ecosystem through the GENIUS Act\054 and Dubai has attracted global firms using regulatory clarity and tax incentives as competitive weapons\054 South Korea also needs strategic regulatory design oriented toward fostering the crypto industry\056 It is worth beginning with a review of spot virtual asset ETF introduction\054 derivatives regulation reform\054 and tax clarification\056 On spot ETFs alone\072 following the U\056S\056 approval in January 2024\054 more than one million BTC in institutional capital including BlackRock has flowed into domestically regulated financial infrastructure\05658\051 This created the structural conditions for expanding asset management revenues and the tax base\056 Singapore established a comprehensive licensing framework for stablecoin issuers in 2023 and moved to full implementation in 2025 \024 an approach assessed as strategic regulatory design aimed at attracting global crypto firms and capital\054 going beyond the defensive objective of investor protection\05659\051 The phenomenon of Korean investors moving funds to overseas exchanges can be seen as the accumulated result of this institutional gap\056\n\nThe third direction is the cultivation of specialist talent in blockchain analytics technology and the fostering of a related corporate ecosystem domestically\056 Chainalysis\054 in its early days            \176)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 28139 0 R
